<attribute name="presentationName" nodeId="d01755b3">Conduct sprint planning</attribute>
<attribute name="briefDescription" nodeId="ffdbea7d"></attribute>
<attribute name="suppressed" nodeId="83f188f6">false</attribute>
<attribute name="orderingGuide" nodeId="8c10366e"></attribute>
<attribute name="authors" nodeId="906fcfe7"></attribute>
<attribute name="changeDate" nodeId="b159db15">Fri Feb 14 10:42:03 EST 2025</attribute>
<attribute name="changeDescription" nodeId="489674e7">MAR 2018 - Baseline</attribute>
<attribute name="version" nodeId="c251c57"></attribute>
<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="37bed5b6">&lt;p&gt;
    Hold a sprint planning session at the start of every sprint. Include the Scrum team, Scrum Master and Product Owner in
    the sprint planning meeting. The Scrum Master facilitates the meeting to&amp;nbsp;so that&amp;nbsp;it occurs and all
    participants understand its purpose. The Product Owner shares the sprint goal for the upcoming sprint followed by a
    presentation of the candidate&amp;nbsp;Backlog Items&amp;nbsp;for the sprint.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Discuss the Backlog Items with the Development Team to forecast how many candidate Backlog Items can be completed in
    the upcoming sprint. This can be done in a number of ways, as outlined in more detail&amp;nbsp;in &lt;a class=&quot;elementLink&quot;
    href=&quot;./../../practice.mgmt.proj.agile_pm.base/guidances/guidelines/sprint_planning_approaches_BAC6EA9F.html&quot;
    guid=&quot;_EM274EJ3EeihzMZlEEReuw&quot;&gt;Sprint Planning Approaches&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;with some of the most common being:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Capacity based planning
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Velocity based planning - more difficult to implement in the case of newly created teams
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Commitment based planning
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    The Development Team may decide to assign tasks to its members during sprint planning. In this event, capacity can be
    tracked separately for each team member. If tasks are not assigned during sprint planning then assignment should
    typically be done on a daily basis, such as during the daily Scrum meeting.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is important&amp;nbsp;to have&amp;nbsp;the following items for this effort to be successful:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Refined Product Backlog
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Latest Product Increment
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Projected team capacity (for capacity based planning)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Development Team&apos;s past performance (if available as performance metrics are typically not available for newly
        formed or young Development Teams)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    If the team determines they are unable to complete all of the candidate Backlog Items, then discuss with the Product
    Owner&amp;nbsp;which subset will bring the most value when accomplished during the sprint. Update, or if not&amp;nbsp;yet done
    create, the Burndown Chart to reflect the work selected for the sprint.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="4dab5840"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="3673d615">&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Sprint planning must occur at the beginning of each sprint.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The Development Team can consider reserving time within the sprint to work on removing inefficiencies in the
        product due to incremental and iterative work (if agreed upon&amp;nbsp;with the Product Owner).
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Lack of a sufficient Product Backlog refinement can lead to prolonged and difficult sprint planning sessions.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Capacity based planning is to be used by newly formed and inexperienced teams.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Velocity based planning is to be used by mature and experienced teams.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Either velocity or Backlog Item estimates can be used by the Product Owner to select the appropriate number of
        candidate Backlog Items, regardless of the approach taken by the Development Team to estimate.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Commitment based planning should be exercised with care when the Development Team does not have much experience
        building the given Product or service.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        If scope adjustments are required over the course of the sprint, eliminate low priority items first as agreed upon
        with the Product Owner.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Scrum Master is responsible for encouraging team members to actively participate in Backlog Item discussions.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Assigning tasks during sprint planning can result in decreased participation as team members will be less
        interested in any tasks not assigned to them. Whereas lack of task assignment may lead to increased participation
        and a better shared understanding of all tasks, as a task could fall to any team member during the sprint.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="690bccec"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="98bb2bb3">To forecast what can be achieved during the sprint and how it can be achieved.</attribute>
<attribute name="alternatives" nodeId="6e4b61e7"></attribute>
